rig_count_tracker.ipynb
Source  : Baker Hughes website (weekly Excel download)
Release : Every Friday ~1:00pm ET
Why     : Leading indicator for US production 6-9 months forward
          Oil rigs drive crude output; gas rigs drive supply
          Permian share tells you where growth is concentrated

Cell 1 — Imports and configuration
Standard imports plus openpyxl for Excel parsing and io for in-memory file handling. No API key needed. Key constants: Baker Hughes North America Excel URL, 
the sheet names we expect to find, and a MANUAL_FALLBACK_PATH pointing to data/BH_rig_count_manual.xlsx — used if the auto-download fails. 
Also imports yfinance for the WTI overlay.

Cell 2 — Download Baker Hughes Excel workbook
Attempts to download the BH North America rotary rig count Excel directly from their website using requests with browser-like headers. 
Saves the raw bytes to data/BH_rig_count_latest.xlsx as a local cache. If the download fails (URL changed, server unreachable), 
falls back to the manual file path with clear instructions. Prints all sheet names found so you can verify the workbook structure.

Cell 3 — Parse US weekly rig count by type
Parses the US sheet to extract the weekly time series of total, oil-directed, gas-directed, and miscellaneous rig counts. 
The BH workbook has an unconventional wide-format layout — dates run across columns, rig categories run down rows — so parsing requires transposing and cleaning carefully. Prints the last 8 weeks as a sanity check.

Cell 4 — Parse basin-level rig count
Parses the US Basin sheet to extract weekly rig counts by major play — Permian, Eagle Ford, Marcellus/Utica, Haynesville, DJ/Niobrara, Williston/Bakken, and Anadarko/SCOOP-STACK. These are the seven basins that account for roughly 85% of US directed drilling activity. Maps BH's column names to clean display labels.

Cell 5 — Fetch WTI price history for overlay
Reuses the WTI price cache from Tools 2 and 4. Short cell — just loads the cache, filters to the relevant date range, and resamples to weekly for alignment with the weekly rig count data.

Cell 6 — Merge and compute analytics
Merges weekly rig count with weekly WTI price using merge_asof. Computes rolling 4-week and 52-week averages for total and oil rig counts. Calculates YoY changes. Adds a Permian share column (Permian rigs as % of total oil rigs) — this is the key concentration metric. Computes the lagged correlation between WTI price and oil rig count (WTI leads by approximately 16–26 weeks based on historical data).

Cell 7 — Four-panel chart
Four panels:

Panel 1 (wide, top): Total rig count history from 2022 onward, stacked by oil/gas/misc, with WTI on a secondary y-axis. Annotates key events (COVID collapse, 2022 price spike, current level).
Panel 2 (bottom-left): Oil-directed rig count with 4-week rolling average — the most watched single number.
Panel 3 (bottom-right): Basin breakdown horizontal bar chart for the latest week, color-coded by commodity type.

Cell 8 — Signal dashboard and plain English summary
Prints: current total/oil/gas rig counts, WoW and YoY changes, 52-week range, Permian concentration, lagged WTI-rig correlation, production outlook signal (rising/stable/falling), and a plain English paragraph connecting rig count to forward production expectations. Cross-tool context from Tool 4 (current WTI price and backwardation signal — the two inputs that most directly drive rig count decisions).

Cell 9 — Save data and wrap up
Saves merged rig count and WTI dataset to CSV. Documents the BH URL maintenance procedure and the monthly code update workflow. Prints next release date (Friday) and the complete cross-tool refresh schedule for all six tools.


Practical note on Baker Hughes URL maintenance. The BH website occasionally changes the direct download URL for their Excel files. The URL in Cell 1 is current as of April 2026 but may change. If the auto-download fails, the manual fallback is the safety net: download the file yourself from bakerhughes.com/rig-count, save it as BH_rig_count_manual.xlsx in your data/ folder, and the notebook will use it automatically. Update the URL as needed. Cell 2 prints clear instructions if the download fails.